  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1120 PM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nA rain band has set up this evening along the 850-mb frontal \naxis stretching from Red Cloud, Nebraska north through Columbus \nand Wayne. Though we are seeing echoes on radar as far east as\nLincoln and Omaha, low-level dry air won't allow anything\nmeasurable to reach the ground. Rain should mostly stay west of\nLincoln and Omaha overnight into Monday morning. \n\nA weak low pressure system rides northeast along the mid-level\nfrontal boundary into eastern Nebraska Monday with chances for \nshowers and storms staying mostly along the stationary boundary \nfrom south-central through northeast Nebraska. Occasional light \nshowers as far east as Lincoln and Fremont through Onawa, IA \nwill be possible. Temperatures south and east of this boundary \nwill stay warm again this afternoon with highs back up in the \n70s to near 80 far south. Areas seeing rain will only see highs \nin the 50s to low 60s. \n\nAs the upper-level trough shifts off to our northeast on\nTuesday, subtle height rises and a weak surface High will bring\na break in the rain for most of our area through Tuesday. An\nopen wave trough moving across Kansas and Missouri may clip far\nsoutheast Nebraska and southwest Iowa with a few showers. Rain\nchances 20-30% starting Tuesday morning into the afternoon.\n\nMore weak disturbances ride along the baroclinic southwesterly\nflow regime Wednesday and Thursday with low confidence in timing\nand coverage. After a slightly cooler day on Tuesday, highs\nwarm back into the upper 60s to mid 70s for Wednesday and \nThursday. A more substantial rain threat is setting up starting \nFriday as a another compact shortwave moves through. With this \nwe see rain chances increase to 40 to 60 percent across much of \nour area Friday afternoon into Saturday morning. Rain chances \n20-30% linger through the weekend as models keep a fairly active\npattern across the region.",
